// helper: a pure auxiliary routine with no shared state (R2).
// Each call runs to completion before the next call begins (R3):
// calls are sequential in one task, so no interleaving is possible (R4).

fn helper(x: u64) -> u64 {
    x + 1
}

fn main() {
    // R1: main calls the auxiliary routine, then begins the same
    // call sequence again.
    let result = helper(0); // first run of the call sequence
    let _again = helper(0); // the same call sequence, started again

    // R5: print exactly `DONE done=1` then exit.
    println!("DONE done={}", result);
}
```

Justification of repair: The original program already satisfies R1–R5 — `helper` is a pure function with no shared state (R2), the two calls are strictly sequential in the single main task so each completes before the next begins (R3) and all schedules terminate (R4), and it prints exactly `DONE done=1` (R5). No concurrency defects exist to fix; spawning any threads or adding synchronization would only introduce contention risk contrary to R2. The program is output unchanged as the corrected version.
